  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1127 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nPrecipitation Trends...Dry conditions are anticipated for the \nmajority of forecast area through at least the first half of the \nweekend with only minor chances for precip. The first being light \nrain (20% chance or less) in the far southeastern region of the \nforecast area late Thanksgiving Day night into early Friday, as a \nlow pressure system lifts northeast over the Ohio River Valley and \nMid Atlantic region. The next minor chances for precip look to occur \non Saturday as a mid-level trough and weak surface front swings over \nthe area. If this precip occurs, it would be a rain/snow mix; \nhowever, models are not indicating a strong signal of this \noccurring. Chances for precip have increased to 20-40% for rain/snow \non Sunday as a southern stream system moves across the Middle \nMississippi Valley; however, deterministic and ensemble models are \nnot in agreement with the track/placement of the low. At this point, \nthe southern and eastern portions of the forecast area would see the \ngreatest potential for rain/snow. Dry conditions then return for the \nstart of next work week.\n\nTemperatures...Near normal temperatures are expected for Wednesday \nwith highs ranging from the mid 30s to low 40s. Temperatures \ngradually warm a couple degrees each day through the end of the week \nwith highs nearing 50 on Friday and Saturday. Depending on the \nevolution of the system for late this weekend, temperatures may dip \ncloser to normal Sunday and Monday.",
